nss-drv: bound the firmware-supplied indices in dynamic_interface and edma stats

Two response handlers use a value the firmware wrote as an array index
with no range test.

nss_dynamic_interface.c indexes
nss_dynamic_interface_assigned_types[NSS_CORE_MAX][NSS_MAX_DYNAMIC_INTERFACES]
- 2 x 128 four-byte enums in .bss - at if_num - NSS_DYNAMIC_IF_START. The
ALLOC arm tests only the sign, and the DEALLOC arm tests nothing at all,
so an if_num of 1..27 stores before the array and anything from 156
upwards stores past it, at a fully scaled offset. On the ALLOC arm the
stored value is firmware-chosen too. The file already carries the right
predicate - nss_is_dynamic_interface() - and applies it on the transmit
side and in nss_dynamic_interface_get_type(), which performs the
identical subscript; only the response handler skips it. Use it in both
arms.

nss_edma_stats.c takes both loop bounds straight from the message:

	for (i = nepss->start_port; i < nepss->end_port; i++)
		edma_stats.port[i]... = nepss->port_stats[j]...;

port_stats[] is a flexible array, so j walks off the response buffer
while i walks off port[NSS_EDMA_NUM_PORTS_MAX]. The header already states
the intended subset size, NSS_EDMA_STATS_MSG_MAX_PORTS, and nothing in
the tree referenced it. Reject a range that is empty, ends past the array
or is wider than one message can carry - a bad pair means the message is
junk, so there is nothing to salvage by clamping.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

